\description{
A  dataset of  songs with their acoustic features.
The data combine AcousticBrainz audio analyses with MusicBrainz canonical recording metadata.
The data was filtered to only contain a select number of artists and only up to 25 songs per artist.
}
\details{
\strong{Selection and matching}

Artists were selected using the preparation file
\code{artists-to-select.csv} found in the R package data-raw folder.
MusicBrainz artist credits were matched to this list using
case-insensitive matching after trimming leading and trailing whitespace.
When multiple metadata rows were available for a recording, the row with
the highest metadata score was retained.

Original recording identifiers were linked to canonical recording
identifiers using the MusicBrainz canonical recording redirect table.
Recordings were retained only when matching metadata and matching rows in
all three AcousticBrainz feature tables (low-level, rhythm, and tonal)
were available.

\strong{Cleaning and aggregation}

Only AcousticBrainz submissions with \code{submission_offset == 0}
were retained. Analyses with missing or nonfinite values in any of
the selected numeric acoustic features were removed before aggregation.

When multiple original recordings mapped to the same canonical recording,
numeric acoustic features were combined using medians.
The character variables \code{key} and \code{scale} were taken from the
first retained analysis and are retained for descriptive interpretation.

Up to 25 canonical recordings per matched artist were randomly selected
without replacement, using seed 20260925. Artists with fewer than
25 eligible recordings contribute all eligible recordings; artists
with no eligible recordings are absent.

\strong{Use and interpretation}

Acoustic features are algorithmic estimates rather than human
assessments. The features have not been standardized in this dataset.
Consider their different units, scales, and meanings before applying
distance-based clustering.

Identifiers, artist and release information, song titles, and
\code{n_acoustic_recordings} provide context or provenance and should not
be treated as acoustic clustering features. In particular,
\code{n_acoustic_recordings} describes how the dataset was constructed,
not a characteristic of how a song sounds.

The variables \code{key} and \code{scale} are categorical musical
descriptors. They may be used to interpret results but should not be
supplied directly to k-means, which requires numeric input.

The artist list was deliberately selected for teaching. The dataset
is not a representative sample of all music, artists, or recordings.
The date of the AcousticBrainz dump indicates when the data were
compiled, not when the music was recorded or released.
}
